In Python an if‑else statement lets you run different code depending on whether a condition is true or false. The syntax starts with the keyword if followed by a condition and a colon; the indented block under it runs when the condition is true. After that you can add an else clause, also ending with a colon, whose indented block runs when the condition is false. For example:

if a > b:
 print("a is greater than b")
else:
 print("b is greater than a or they are equal")

If you need to test more than two possibilities you can chain additional checks with elif (short for "else if"):

if a > b:
 print("a is greater than b")
elif b > a:
 print("b is greater than a")
else:
 print("a and b are equal")

Thus, if‑else provides a basic way to control the flow of a program based on conditions.
